WEBVTT

00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:02.958
You ask whether open access increases

00:00:02.978 --> 00:00:06.476
citations. An LLM writes a plausible

00:00:06.496 --> 00:00:09.620
paragraph about a study comparing open and

00:00:09.640 --> 00:00:11.863
subscription articles while accounting for

00:00:11.883 --> 00:00:14.207
discipline and publication year.

00:00:14.383 --> 00:00:16.515
No identified study supports that

00:00:16.535 --> 00:00:19.028
paragraph. Could the invented text still

00:00:19.048 --> 00:00:21.372
help a search? Pause and predict.

00:00:23.794 --> 00:00:25.509
It can serve as a retrieval aid.

00:00:25.671 --> 00:00:28.886
Query2doc and HyDE use generated language

00:00:28.906 --> 00:00:31.476
in different ways to find real indexed

00:00:31.496 --> 00:00:34.478
sources. Neither method turns the imagined

00:00:34.498 --> 00:00:37.713
paragraph into evidence, or establishes

00:00:37.733 --> 00:00:39.851
that the described study exists.

00:00:40.750 --> 00:00:44.129
Query2doc generates a pseudo document and

00:00:44.149 --> 00:00:46.436
uses its language to expand the original

00:00:46.456 --> 00:00:49.400
query. The additional vocabulary may

00:00:49.420 --> 00:00:51.552
resemble wording found in relevant

00:00:51.572 --> 00:00:54.310
documents. The expanded input can go to a

00:00:54.330 --> 00:00:57.853
sparse retriever such as BM twenty five.

00:00:58.417 --> 00:01:01.103
Dense retrieval is also possible; the

00:01:01.123 --> 00:01:03.538
diagram follows the lexical route.

00:01:03.701 --> 00:01:05.266
Its effect depends on what the next

00:01:05.286 --> 00:01:07.314
retriever does with those words.

00:01:08.212 --> 00:01:09.875
HyDE means Hypothetical Document

00:01:09.895 --> 00:01:12.697
Embeddings. An LLM generates a

00:01:12.717 --> 00:01:15.210
hypothetical relevant document, then a

00:01:15.230 --> 00:01:18.431
dense encoder embeds that document.

00:01:18.594 --> 00:01:20.919
Retrieval compares the resulting vector

00:01:20.939 --> 00:01:23.509
with stored vectors for real indexed

00:01:23.529 --> 00:01:24.488
documents.

00:01:25.044 --> 00:01:27.384
The generated document is a pivot into the

00:01:27.404 --> 00:01:30.297
vector space. It is not the final answer

00:01:30.317 --> 00:01:31.881
or a verified source.

00:01:32.044 --> 00:01:34.935
Unlike Query2doc’s language expansion,

00:01:35.123 --> 00:01:37.462
this path uses the generated document’s

00:01:37.482 --> 00:01:39.613
embedding to conduct the search.

00:01:40.536 --> 00:01:42.407
The chapter’s illustrative paragraph

00:01:42.427 --> 00:01:44.984
mentions comparisons and adjustment for

00:01:45.004 --> 00:01:46.620
discipline and year.

00:01:46.795 --> 00:01:49.842
Those details may steer retrieval towards

00:01:49.862 --> 00:01:52.857
particular vocabulary or study designs.

00:01:53.033 --> 00:01:55.203
They are not findings about an identified

00:01:55.223 --> 00:01:57.317
paper, and may import assumptions the

00:01:57.337 --> 00:01:59.262
searcher did not intend.

00:01:59.807 --> 00:02:02.199
Retrieving real sources does not

00:02:02.219 --> 00:02:05.175
retroactively make every generated detail

00:02:05.195 --> 00:02:08.912
true. Read and assess the actual sources,

00:02:09.036 --> 00:02:10.910
and retain the generated text in the

00:02:10.930 --> 00:02:13.217
search record. It is part of the retrieval

00:02:13.237 --> 00:02:15.832
strategy, not supporting evidence.

00:02:16.728 --> 00:02:19.083
The chapter’s product accounts describe

00:02:19.129 --> 00:02:20.449
different receivers.

00:02:20.598 --> 00:02:24.998
EBSCOhost and EDS AI Assisted Search parse

00:02:25.018 --> 00:02:26.918
keyword and noun phrases for the

00:02:26.938 --> 00:02:29.393
established engine; the cited public page

00:02:29.413 --> 00:02:31.970
does not identify the parsing model as an

00:02:31.990 --> 00:02:32.768
LLM.

00:02:33.335 --> 00:02:35.668
Web of Science Smart Search combines

00:02:35.688 --> 00:02:38.335
structured parsing with keyword and vector

00:02:38.355 --> 00:02:39.572
document paths.

00:02:39.734 --> 00:02:42.369
Primo NDE documents generative Boolean

00:02:42.389 --> 00:02:44.546
construction for Advanced Search.

00:02:44.683 --> 00:02:46.982
These pinned documentation accounts are

00:02:47.002 --> 00:02:49.573
not fresh service tests; the shared

00:02:49.593 --> 00:02:51.931
interface language does not identify a

00:02:51.951 --> 00:02:53.348
common architecture.

00:02:54.239 --> 00:02:56.513
Natural language filter extraction is

00:02:56.533 --> 00:02:58.394
useful but partial.

00:02:58.557 --> 00:03:01.990
Primo NDE recognises a defined list of

00:03:02.010 --> 00:03:05.006
fields and facets, with documented author

00:03:05.026 --> 00:03:07.300
detection failures and unintended OR

00:03:07.320 --> 00:03:09.877
relationships. Web of Science Research

00:03:09.897 --> 00:03:12.686
Assistant supports some institution,

00:03:12.706 --> 00:03:15.276
geography and date constraints.

00:03:15.832 --> 00:03:17.986
That Research Assistant’s guidance also

00:03:18.006 --> 00:03:21.361
lists complex field tagged Boolean queries

00:03:21.382 --> 00:03:22.856
as unsupported.

00:03:23.006 --> 00:03:25.486
Supported natural language patterns do not

00:03:25.506 --> 00:03:28.269
replace the database’s complete advanced

00:03:28.289 --> 00:03:29.390
search language.

00:03:29.565 --> 00:03:32.109
Keep product features distinct and inspect

00:03:32.129 --> 00:03:34.403
the actual generated conditions.

00:03:35.296 --> 00:03:37.535
A filter inferred from the question is

00:03:37.555 --> 00:03:39.455
different from one the user selects

00:03:39.475 --> 00:03:42.148
explicitly. Primo Research Assistant

00:03:42.168 --> 00:03:45.008
documents limited automatic refinements;

00:03:45.042 --> 00:03:47.148
manually changing a refinement switches

00:03:47.168 --> 00:03:49.803
that field to manual handling, so it is no

00:03:49.823 --> 00:03:52.160
longer inferred automatically.

00:03:52.712 --> 00:03:55.375
Test which supported phrasings activate

00:03:55.395 --> 00:03:57.476
each constraint, whether the generated

00:03:57.496 --> 00:04:00.208
filter is visible, and what happens with

00:04:00.228 --> 00:04:02.604
unsupported or ambiguous limits.

00:04:02.767 --> 00:04:05.143
Compare variants against known relevant

00:04:05.163 --> 00:04:07.837
records rather than assuming a fluent

00:04:07.857 --> 00:04:09.834
request was fully obeyed.

00:04:10.744 --> 00:04:13.309
Keep the original input, generated text,

00:04:13.329 --> 00:04:15.809
structured expressions, inferred and

00:04:15.829 --> 00:04:18.747
manual filters, chosen routes, and

00:04:18.767 --> 00:04:21.053
intermediate results used for feedback.

00:04:21.216 --> 00:04:23.812
A compulsory clause or hidden route can

00:04:23.832 --> 00:04:26.930
exclude evidence before ranking; an early

00:04:26.950 --> 00:04:29.597
mistaken ranking can steer the next query.

00:04:30.150 --> 00:04:32.621
Understanding interprets, transformation

00:04:32.641 --> 00:04:35.598
changes inputs, and control decides where

00:04:35.618 --> 00:04:38.021
they go. All can occur in a prescribed

00:04:38.041 --> 00:04:40.688
workflow. The next chapter moves to the

00:04:40.708 --> 00:04:43.137
separate question of who chooses what

00:04:43.157 --> 00:04:45.469
happens next while the search is running.
